**Parity with HORIZON Arm A reproduced exactly; first admissible updates were cosine `1e-3`: 3,200, step `1e-3`: 2,200, inverse-time (`tau=1000`) `1e-3`: `NONE`, plateau (`n=3`) `1e-3`: 2,800, and the step `3e-3` extension: 2,300; estimated `16 seeds x 5 arms` costs for the admissible schedules are cosine `0.47–0.55`, step `1e-3` `0.28–0.32`, plateau `0.36–0.40`, and step `3e-3` `0.32–0.35` CPU-hours.**

`DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_CLAIM` — development material only.

## Answer

[VERIFIED BY RUNNING CODE] Three of the four schedules starting at the current
`1e-3` literal reached the unchanged convergence criterion before the
4,000-update cap. The required higher-start extension also reached it.
Inverse-time did not. These are all the admissible schedules; this diagnostic
does not name a winner or select a production schedule.

| schedule | starting rate | fixed parameter | first admissible update | all four checks at cap |
|:---|---:|:---|---:|:---:|
| cosine | `1e-3` | endpoint zero at 4,000 | **3,200** | yes |
| step decay | `1e-3` | factor 0.1 after 2,000 and after 3,000 | **2,200** | yes |
| inverse-time | `1e-3` | `tau=1000` | **NONE** | no |
| plateau decay | `1e-3` | factor 0.5, `n=3` | **2,800** | yes |
| step-decay extension | `3e-3` | same step shape | **2,300** | yes |

[VERIFIED BY RUNNING CODE] Inverse-time passes three of four components at
4,000 and still returns `NONE`. Its objective motion is **1.9460%** against a
1% tolerance, an excess of **0.9460 percentage points** (or 0.009460 in the
relative-motion quantity). Its R2, ordering, and top-1 motions are 0.005363,
0.002967, and 0, within their unchanged 0.01, 0.005, and 0.005 tolerances.

Loss and held-out metrics here are convergence diagnostics only. **No EE
quantity was computed, and neither EE nor predictive-metric level selected
anything.**

## Parity gate

[VERIFIED BY RUNNING CODE] Before fitting a decay schedule, a fresh
constant-`1e-3` run reproduced HORIZON Arm A and the original LRSWEEP C3 cell
exactly at every checkpoint from 0 through 500. Equality covered the training
objective and the complete held-out metric dictionaries, not rounded display
values.

| update | objective | held-out R2 | ordering | top-1 |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 0 | 131.376445 | -2.555855 | 0.606666 | 0.000000 |
| 100 | 2.144771 | 0.698493 | 0.552962 | 0.333333 |
| 200 | 1.769994 | 0.677553 | 0.565035 | 0.250000 |
| 300 | 1.545214 | 0.646160 | 0.560110 | 0.166667 |
| 400 | 1.370980 | 0.618354 | 0.563186 | 0.166667 |
| 500 | 1.248175 | 0.597967 | 0.532182 | 0.250000 |

## Unchanged rule and schedule definitions

[VERIFIED BY RUNNING CODE] At every destination cadence `k=100,...,4000`, the
runner applied LRSWEEP's rule to `k-100 -> k`: objective relative absolute
motion `<=0.01`, level-R2 absolute motion `<=0.01`, ordering absolute motion
`<=0.005`, and top-1 absolute motion `<=0.005`. All four checks and finite
objective/parameters were required together.

[VERIFIED BY RUNNING CODE] All runs used C3, the twelve fixed LRSWEEP anchors
and identical LOAO folds, model seed `V025_LEARNER/seed/1`
(`6407676579069309528`), the sealed `(64,64)` ReLU head, every other sealed
Adam literal, full-batch gradient aggregation, and cadence 100.

[DERIVED ON PAPER, FIXED BEFORE RUNNING] With one-indexed update `k` and
`t=k-1`, the smooth schedules were:

- cosine: `lr(t)=lr0*(1+cos(pi*t/4000))/2`, with the schedule endpoint set to
  zero after update 4,000;
- inverse-time: `lr(t)=lr0/(1+t/1000)`, hence `tau=1000`;
- step: updates 1–2,000 at `lr0`, 2,001–3,000 at `0.1*lr0`, and 3,001–4,000
  at `0.01*lr0`;
- plateau: factor 0.5 after `n=3` consecutive cadence motions that do not set
  a new best objective-motion value, with patience reset after each decay.

[VERIFIED BY RUNNING CODE] Plateau decayed after updates 1,700, 2,200, 2,700,
3,200, and 3,700, ending at `3.125e-5`. Decisions used the aggregate LOAO
training objective and affected only subsequent updates.

[DERIVED ON PAPER, FIXED BEFORE RUNNING] The mandated higher start was
`3e-3`, the next higher rate in LRSWEEP's already-fixed grid. Step shape was
assigned to that extension because its base run had the earliest
first-admissible cadence under the frozen extension-ranking rule (2,200,
versus plateau 2,800 and cosine 3,200; inverse-time was `NONE`). That assignment
uses convergence timing only; it is not a predictive-quality or production
selection. The extension first became admissible at 2,300, so increasing the
start did not produce an earlier admissible cadence in this diagnostic.

## Motions at the cap

[VERIFIED BY RUNNING CODE] The last five objective motions are for destinations
