## Wall time and production extrapolation

[VERIFIED BY RUNNING CODE] Wall seconds per 100 updates are end-to-end cap
wall divided by 40 for the complete twelve-fold, one-seed diagnostic. The
cadence-timed means differ by less than 0.001 s from the displayed values.

| schedule | cap wall | wall s/100 updates |
|:---|---:|---:|
| cosine `1e-3` | 328.47 s | 8.212 |
| step `1e-3` | 282.28 s | 7.057 |
| inverse-time `1e-3` | 288.04 s | 7.201 |
| plateau `1e-3` | 287.35 s | 7.184 |
| step `3e-3` extension | 306.44 s | 7.661 |

[DERIVED ON PAPER] A `16 seeds x 5 arms` production set contains 80 C3 heads.
The lower estimate uses mean update-only time per LOAO fold through the first
admissible cadence. The upper estimate uses cumulative end-to-end per-fold
time through that cadence and multiplies by `12/11` for production training
on all twelve anchors rather than each fold's eleven. Both assume serial
single-thread execution, the same dimensions, no shared-head speedup, and
negligible orchestration/serialization.

| admissible schedule | first update | production CPU-hours | updates versus current 500 |
|:---|---:|---:|---:|
| cosine `1e-3` | 3,200 | 0.47–0.55 | 6.4x |
| step `1e-3` | 2,200 | 0.28–0.32 | 4.4x |
| plateau `1e-3` | 2,800 | 0.36–0.40 | 5.6x |
| step `3e-3` extension | 2,300 | 0.32–0.35 | 4.6x |

[DERIVED ON PAPER] Yes, four tested runs reach admissibility before 4,000, so
stopping at the first admissible cadence is cheaper than taking those runs to
the 4,000-update cap. **No tested schedule reaches admissibility by update
500.** Therefore none is cheaper than the current 500-update configuration;
the earliest requires 2,200 updates, or 4.4 times as many. Reaching before
4,000 and being cheaper than 500 are not equivalent comparisons.

